{
  "requirement": "The SDK offers this `InspectionContext` method. The supplied `ToolAgent`\nalso exposes the `read_node` command.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<'d, D: Contents> InspectionContext<'d, D> {\n    /// Read the contents of this graph node.\n    pub fn read(&self, node: &D::NodeId) -> Result<D::NodeContent, D::Error>;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Method signature.** The requirement asks for `InspectionContext<'d, D: Contents>::read(&self, node: &D::NodeId) -> Result<D::NodeContent, D::Error>`. `crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs#impl[InspectionContext<'d, D>]/fn[read]` has this signature. It sits in `impl<'d, D: Contents> InspectionContext<'d, D>`, and the struct is declared as `InspectionContext<'d, D: Contents>`. The method is `pub` and takes `&self` and `node: &D::NodeId`. It returns `Result<D::NodeContent, D::Error>`. It delegates to `read_snapshot` and returns the snapshot's `content`, which has type `D::NodeContent`. `Contents` is a trait extending `Graph` that declares `NodeContent`.\n\n**`read_node` command on the `ToolAgent`.** `crates/habeas-sdk/src/agent/tools.rs#impl[GraphSession<'_, '_, D>/Conversation]/fn[tools]` advertises a tool named `read_node`. Its parameters schema requires a `path` made of `segments`. In the same impl, `fn[dispatch]` handles the `\"read_node\"` name. It resolves the node, reads it through the inspection context, formats the content, and returns it as pages. `fn[run]` of the `ToolAgent` also names `read_node` in its prompt. I did not open `GraphSession`'s construction, so I did not confirm directly that `ToolAgent` is the type that builds it. The prompt text and the `tools`/`dispatch` pairing make that linkage clear.\n\n**Result.** Both structural obligations are met. I assessed no refining declaration and found no gaps. There are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments, and none are needed.",
